{'title':'Sorting II: mergesort, quicksort, lower bounds, linear sorting — and solving recurrences','lectures':[8,9],
 'summary':'Split problems into smaller parts and trace how the results combine. Solve the recurrences that describe them, explain the comparison lower bound, and see how restricted integer keys permit linear sorting.',
 'objectives':['Trace merge and partition on small arrays and count the key comparisons.','Derive the mergesort recurrence and solve it with a recursion tree or the master theorem.','Compare quicksort’s best, worst and expected cases and say what a random pivot guarantees.','Prove the Ω(n log n) comparison lower bound from the decision tree.','Trace bucketsort and radix sort and state the assumptions that make them linear.'],
 'scope':['Mergesort: trace, recurrence T(n)=2T(n/2)+Θ(n), Θ(n log n)','Solving recurrences: recursion trees and the master theorem (core)','Quicksort: partition, pivot choice, worst case Θ(n²), expected O(n log n) with random pivots','Decision-tree lower bound Ω(n log n) for comparison sorting','Bucketsort and LSD radix sort: stability, cost O(d(n+b)), assumptions','Is quicksort really faster than heapsort? (reading only)'],
 'examples':[ex('Where does n log n come from?',
 'Merge the sorted runs [1,4,7] and [2,3,8].',
 ['Compare the front values: choose 1, then 2, then 3.','Compare 4 with 8: choose 4. Compare 7 with 8: choose 7.','The first run is empty; append 8 without another key comparison. Five comparisons merge six items.','Mergesort handles n items per level across about log₂n levels: Θ(n log n).'],
 'Quicksort can split into sizes 0 and n−1 repeatedly, giving Θ(n²). Random pivots give an expected O(n log n) bound, not a worst-case guarantee.',figure=7),
 ex('How can a linear-time sort coexist with an n log n lower bound?',
 'Stable radix-sort [21,13,12,23] by units, then tens.',
 ['Units pass: [21,12,13,23]. Equal units preserve input order.','Tens pass: [12,13,21,23]. Equal tens preserve the units order established earlier.','Each stable counting pass costs O(n+b) for base b. With d digits the total is O(d(n+b)).','The Ω(n log n) worst-case lower bound concerns comparison-only sorting of arbitrary distinct keys. Radix sort uses key digits.'],
 'The digit count and range matter. Calling radix sort O(n) without stating these assumptions is incomplete.',figure=8)],
 'python':'A recursive function calls itself on a smaller input and must have a stopping case. Trace the calls as a tree first. Use // for integer quotient and % for remainder to isolate a digit.',
 'code':'def merge(a, b):\n    out = []\n    i = j = 0\n    while i < len(a) and j < len(b):\n        if a[i] <= b[j]:\n            out.append(a[i]); i += 1\n        else:\n            out.append(b[j]); j += 1\n    return out + a[i:] + b[j:]\nprint(merge([1, 4, 7], [2, 3, 8]))  # [1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8]\n\nvalues = [21, 13, 12, 23]\n# sorted is stable; these lines demonstrate digit order,\n# not a linear-time counting-sort implementation.\nvalues = sorted(values, key=lambda x: x % 10)\nprint(values)  # [21, 12, 13, 23]\nvalues = sorted(values, key=lambda x: x // 10)\nprint(values)  # [12, 13, 21, 23]'},
