{'title':'Graphs II: DFS, edge types, topological order, strongly connected components','lectures':[12],
 'summary':'Trace depth-first search with entry and exit times, classify every edge, and use the classes to detect cycles, order a DAG and find strongly connected components.',
 'objectives':['Trace DFS on a directed graph and record entry and exit times for every vertex.','Classify each edge as tree, back, forward or cross from the times and the vertex states.','Prove that a directed graph has a cycle if and only if DFS meets a back edge.','Derive a topological order from DFS finishing times and check it against every edge.','Compare the DFS and in-degree-removal methods for topological sorting.'],
 'scope':['DFS: recursion, entry/exit times, O(n+m)','Edge types in undirected and directed DFS','Cycle detection by back edges','Topological sorting: reverse finishing order and in-degree-zero removal','Strongly connected components: definition and DFS-based idea','Articulation vertices (reading only)'],
 'examples':[ex('Which edges does DFS turn into tree edges, and what do the others tell us?',
 'Directed edges: A→B, A→C, B→D, C→D, C→F, D→E, E→B. Run DFS from A, taking neighbours in alphabetical order. Classify every edge, then give a topological order or show the cycle.',
 ['Start at A (entry 1). Take the first neighbour B (entry 2), then D (entry 3), then E (entry 4). Tree edges so far: A→B, B→D, D→E.','From E, the edge E→B reaches a vertex that is discovered but not finished: B is still on the recursion stack. Classify E→B as a back edge. Then E finishes (exit 5), D finishes (exit 6) and B finishes (exit 7).','Back at A, take C (entry 8). The edge C→D reaches a finished vertex whose entry time 3 is earlier than 8: a cross edge. The edge C→F opens F (entry 9, exit 10). C finishes at 11 and A at 12.','Result: tree edges A→B, B→D, D→E, A→C, C→F; back edge E→B; cross edge C→D; no forward edge. A back edge closes a directed cycle, here B→D→E→B, so no topological order exists.'],
 'Reverse the edge E→B to B→E. DFS now meets no back edge; B→E becomes a forward edge because E is finished and was entered after B. The finishing order is E, D, B, F, C, A. Reversed, it gives the topological order A, C, F, B, D, E. Check every edge: each points from left to right.',
 figure='dfs')],
 'python':'DFS uses recursion or an explicit stack. Record the entry and exit times in two dictionaries and classify an edge by the state of the vertex it reaches.',
 'code':'g = {"A":["B","C"], "B":["D"], "C":["D","F"], "D":["E"], "E":["B"], "F":[]}\nentry, exit, clock = {}, {}, [0]\ndef dfs(u):\n    clock[0] += 1; entry[u] = clock[0]\n    for v in g[u]:\n        if v not in entry:\n            dfs(v)                      # tree edge\n        elif v not in exit:\n            print("back edge", u, v)     # v still on the stack: a cycle\n    clock[0] += 1; exit[u] = clock[0]\ndfs("A")\nprint(sorted(exit, key=exit.get, reverse=True))  # reverse finishing order'},
{'title':'Minimum spanning trees and union-find','lectures':[13],
 'summary':'Build a minimum-weight tree that connects every vertex. Compare the two greedy orders, Prim and Kruskal, and use union-find to make Kruskal’s cycle test cheap.',
 'objectives':['Trace Prim’s algorithm from a start vertex and Kruskal’s algorithm on a sorted edge list.','Prove the cut property and use it to justify each greedy choice.','Trace union-find with union by size and path compression and bound the cost of find.','Compare the running times of Prim and Kruskal in terms of n and m.','Explain why an MST does not in general contain shortest paths.'],
 'scope':['Spanning trees; weighted graphs','Prim’s algorithm: trace and O(n²) or O(m log n) bound','Kruskal’s algorithm: sort edges, accept if different components, O(m log m)','Cut property and the correctness of greedy MST choices','Union-find: parent array, union by size, path compression, near-constant amortised cost','Uniqueness of the MST when weights are distinct'],
 'examples':[ex('Does any tree connecting all vertices have the smallest total weight?',
 'A triangle has undirected edge weights AB=1, BC=2, AC=3. Apply Kruskal’s algorithm.',
 ['Sort the edges by weight: AB, BC, AC.','Accept AB: it joins different components.','Accept BC: all three vertices are now connected. Total weight is 3.','Reject AC because it closes a cycle. The cut property justifies choosing a lightest edge across a cut.'],
 'An MST minimises total tree weight, not every pairwise path. Here its A-to-C path weighs 3; change AC to 2.5 and the MST stays AB,BC although the direct path is shorter.',figure=10),
 ex('How does Kruskal know, quickly, that an edge would close a cycle?',
 'Elements 1–6 start as six singleton sets, with parent[i]=i and size 1. Perform union(1,2), union(3,4), union(2,4), find(4), union(5,6), union(1,3), using union by size and path compression.',
 ['union(1,2): both roots have size 1; attach 2 under 1. parent[2]=1, size[1]=2. union(3,4): likewise parent[4]=3, size[3]=2.','union(2,4): find(2)=1 and find(4)=3; the sizes are equal, so attach root 3 under root 1. parent[3]=1, size[1]=4. The set is now {1,2,3,4}.','find(4): follow 4→3→1. Path compression then sets parent[4]=1 directly, so the next find(4) takes one step. union(5,6): parent[6]=5.','union(1,3): find(1)=1 and find(3)=1 are the same root, so nothing changes. In Kruskal’s algorithm this is exactly the case where the edge is rejected because it would close a cycle.'],
 'Without union by size, unions in the order (1,2), (2,3), (3,4), … can build a chain and make one find cost Θ(n). Union by size keeps every tree of height at most lg n, so find is O(log n); with path compression the cost per operation is almost constant on average.',
 label='Core: union-find')],
 'python':'Kruskal’s cycle test is two find calls. Keep a parent list and a size list; a root is an element that is its own parent.',
 'code':'parent = list(range(7)); size = [1] * 7   # elements 1..6, index 0 unused\ndef find(x):\n    while parent[x] != x:\n        parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]   # compress the path as we go\n        x = parent[x]\n    return x\ndef union(a, b):\n    ra, rb = find(a), find(b)\n    if ra == rb:\n        return False                    # same set: this edge would close a cycle\n    if size[ra] < size[rb]:\n        ra, rb = rb, ra\n    parent[rb] = ra; size[ra] += size[rb]\n    return True\nfor a, b in [(1, 2), (3, 4), (2, 4), (5, 6), (1, 3)]:\n    print((a, b), union(a, b))\nprint(find(4), parent[1:])  # 1 [1, 1, 1, 1, 5, 5]'},
{'title':'Shortest paths: Dijkstra, Bellman–Ford, Floyd','lectures':[14],
 'summary':'Relax weighted edges and understand the condition behind Dijkstra’s algorithm. Use Bellman–Ford when weights may be negative, and Floyd for every pair at once.',
 'objectives':['Trace Dijkstra’s algorithm with distance and predecessor tables and reconstruct a path.','State the edge-weight condition Dijkstra needs and give a counterexample when it fails.','Trace one Bellman–Ford round and bound the number of rounds needed.','Derive the Floyd update rule and trace it on a three-vertex graph.','Choose among BFS, Dijkstra, Bellman–Ford and Floyd for a stated shortest-path question.'],
 'scope':['Relaxation: d(v)=min(d(v), d(u)+w(u,v)); predecessor tables','Dijkstra’s algorithm: settled-once rule, nonnegative weights, O(n²) or O(m log n)','Bellman–Ford (core): n−1 rounds of relaxing every edge, negative edges, negative-cycle detection','Floyd’s all-pairs algorithm: the k-loop update and O(n³)','Adding a constant to every edge changes the shortest path (warm-up)','Updating an MST after adding one edge (reading only)'],
 'examples':[ex('When is a tentative shortest distance safe to finalise?',
 'Directed edges A→B=2, A→C=5, B→C=1. Find distances from A.',
 ['Initial distances: A=0, B=∞, C=∞.','Settle A and relax outgoing edges: B=2, C=5.','Settle B next; the route A→B→C improves C to 2+1=3.','Settle C. Final distances are 0,2,3; predecessor links reconstruct the paths.'],
 'Dijkstra’s standard settled-once method requires nonnegative weights. With A→B=2, A→C=5, C→B=−4 it can finalise B too early, missing distance 1.',figure=11),
 ex('What does one round of Bellman–Ford guarantee?',
 'Directed edges S→A=4, S→B=2, B→A=−3, A→C=1, B→C=5. Relax every edge once, in the listed order, starting from d(S)=0 and all other distances ∞.',
 ['Relax S→A: d(A)=min(∞, 0+4)=4. Relax S→B: d(B)=min(∞, 0+2)=2.','Relax B→A: d(A)=min(4, 2−3)=−1. The negative edge improves A through B.','Relax A→C: d(C)=min(∞, −1+1)=0. Relax B→C: 2+5=7 does not improve 0. After round 1: S=0, A=−1, B=2, C=0.','Round 2 changes nothing, so the algorithm stops. In general, after round k every shortest path with at most k edges is correct, so n−1=3 rounds always suffice. If round n still changes a distance, a negative cycle is reachable.'],
 'Relax the same edges in the order A→C, B→C, B→A, S→A, S→B. Round 1 then ends with A=4, B=2, C=∞, and it takes three rounds to reach A=−1, C=0. The final answer does not depend on the order; the number of useful rounds does. Dijkstra on this graph settles B (2) before A and still finds −1 here, but with S→A=4, S→B=2, A→B=−3 it settles B at 2 and never corrects it to 1.',
 label='Core: Bellman–Ford',figure='bellman')],
 'python':'Use a distance table and predecessor table. A priority queue chooses the smallest tentative distance. Bellman–Ford needs only a loop over the edge list, repeated n−1 times.','code':11},
